WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.433
How can light
reveal an obstacle

2
00:00:02.433 --> 00:00:03.667
without being absorbed

3
00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:05.300
or reflected by it?

4
00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:08.700
Send one photon
into an interferometer:

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.200
two possible paths,

6
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.200
joined again.

7
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.233
With both paths open

8
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:15.533
and the device
correctly aligned,

9
00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:17.833
interference keeps
one detector dark.

10
00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:20.800
The waves associated
with the two paths

11
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.400
cancel at that output.

12
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:24.800
Now put a
light-absorbing object

13
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.100
in one path.

14
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.300
If it absorbs the photon,

15
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.033
neither output
detector clicks.

16
00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:31.233
But sometimes

17
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:33.233
the formerly dark detector

18
00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:34.233
clicks instead.

19
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.100
That single photon
reached the detector,

20
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.000
so the object
did not absorb it.

21
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.267
The blocked path

22
00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:42.500
removed the cancellation.

23
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.867
The click reveals
the obstacle.

24
00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:47.033
Other trials absorb
the photon

25
00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:49.233
or give no clear answer.

26
00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:50.967
This is not a

27
00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:52.267
guaranteed harmless test.

28
00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:54.933
Researchers have
demonstrated this effect,

29
00:00:54.933 --> 00:00:56.400
called interaction-free
measurement.

30
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.367
The clue comes from

31
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:00.833
changing which paths
are available.
